(project pricklypear-multi-user
 (doc "Pricklypear book chapter: multi-user — MANY HUMANS.
        Cotenant isolation (this chapter) is the shipping model:
        one process, many sessions, isolation by session + branch.
        Process-per-user isolation (multi-user.isolation) is a
        future hardening axis. single-image.borg is about shipping
        ONE binary, not one human. Do not read single-image as
        cancelling multi-user.
        Access surfaces that ship on cotenant first: user subdomains,
        per-user LLM keys, SSH→REPL (auth.user-subdomains /
        user-llm-keys / user-ssh-repl).")
 (status partial)
 (convention ocaml-dune)
 (section multi-user
  (doc "Cotenant isolation (one process, session + branch scoped), namespaces via branch ownership, budgets, author-runs-as-author")
  (status partial)
  
  (subsection cotenant-isolation
   (doc "Session-level isolation in one process — THE v1 model")
   (status implemented)
   (details
    (doc (|
         One image process serves many authenticated sessions.
         Isolation is session + branch scoped, not process
         scoped:

         - Each session carries its own identity (user_id,
           username, is_admin) resolved at login and attached
           per request.
         - Each session has its own default branch
           (<username>/personal); defines, routes, and type
           declarations land on the session's current branch.
         - No session writes to another user's personal
           branch; overrides on a personal branch shadow
           system/main only for that branch's readers.
         - Session state is not shared; a wedged call aborts
           its own session's work, not another user's.

         What cotenant does NOT provide (honest limits):
         - No per-user process boundary: a hard process
           crash takes all sessions down together (the
           watchdog restarts the one image for everyone).
         - Resource contention is shared: one user's
           expensive eval competes with everyone else's
           (mitigated by per-session step budgets — see
           budgets).
         Per-user image processes (multi-user.isolation)
         remain the future hardening axis for a stronger
         blast radius.
    |))
   )
  )
  (subsection isolation
   (doc "Future hardening axis: process-level isolation, one image per user")
   (status deprecated)
   (details
    (doc (|
         DESIGN SKETCH (not the shipping topology).

         The retired fleet design gave each user their OWN
         image process rather than namespacing within one
         shared image — the Unix/POSIX model: each user has
         a home (their branch bindings); logging in boots
         or attaches their image; each image is a separate
         process.

         Wins it would buy: a user's infinite loop or bad
         define wedges only their image; per-user A/B
         rollback; no reliance on cooperative scheduling
         for cross-user safety.

         Why it is not v1: it needs a supervisor/fleet
         layer (boot-on-connect, attach, per-user RAM),
         which the single-image pivot retired. Cotenant
         isolation (above) is the shipping model; this
         remains the future hardening axis if a stronger
         blast radius is ever needed.
    |))
   )
  )
  (subsection namespaces
   (doc "Branch ownership namespaces; packages are a separate in-branch axis")
   (status partial)
   (details
    (doc (|
         TWO AXES (do not conflate):

         1. BRANCH namespace (this subsection):
            There is no privileged 'common' namespace. There
            is 'main' (system baseline) and per-user branches.
            Versioning/ownership IS branch ownership.
            A binding's key is (branch_id, name). When Alice
            forks main to alice/main, she gets her own
            'greet' independent of main's greet (until she
            merges).

         2. PACKAGE prefix (image.packages):
            Within ONE branch, organize functions with `/`
            in the name (todo/list). That is organization,
            not ownership. See image.packages.

         Cross-branch calls (still deferred): a function can
         explicitly reference another branch's binding via a
         qualified name (e.g. system/main:greet — note colon
         or other separator TBD, must not collide with package `/`).
         Normally a function calls bindings in its own branch.

         The 'common namespace problem' is thus reframed
         as the 'merge to main politics problem':
         - Default: admin-only merges to main
         - All users can fork freely
         - Users can merge into their own branches freely
         - Politics expand later (PR/review workflow)

         This sidesteps the up-or-down political decision
         the original 'publish to common' model forced.
   |))
   )
  )
  (subsection budgets
   (doc "Per-call step budgets to bound runaway user code")
   (status partial)
   (verify
    (build "dune build @all")
    (smoke "a Lisp call that exceeds its step budget aborts cleanly; the session survives"))
   (details
    (doc (|
         Each Lisp primitive invocation runs with a step
         counter. When exceeded, that ONE call aborts;
         the session continues. This prevents "one user's
         bad function wedges the whole image" at the
         interpreter level (defense in depth alongside
         process-level isolation).

         Default budget: configurable per session, sensible
         initial value (e.g. 100k eval steps). User can
         request more for legitimate long-running operations
         via a primitive that explicitly raises the budget
         for a sub-call (and logs it).

         The interpreter checks the counter at every Prim
         invocation and every recursive eval depth. Cheap:
         one integer increment per step.

         Memory: also tracked. A session exceeding memory
         budget aborts the offending call. (Deferred; step
         budget is the v1 mechanism.)

         This is per-CALL, not per-session: a session can
         make many calls, each independently bounded.
   |))
   )
  )
  (subsection permissions
   (doc "Author-runs-as-author: functions execute with their author's data perms")
   (status partial)
   (details
    (doc (|
         A function executes with ITS AUTHOR's data
         permissions, not the caller's. This is the Unix
         setuid model: if alice authors a function that
         reads her calendar, and bob calls it, the function
         reads alice's calendar (not bob's), and bob sees
         only what alice's function returns.

         Implications:
         - The interpreter's eval carries a (current-author)
           field, read from the EXECUTED binding's author,
           not the session user.
         - Data primitives (query, get, create, etc.)
           consult current-author for permission filtering.
         - A careless privileged function is a security
           hole, same as Unix setuid. Honest and familiar.
         - Functions can expose capabilities to other
           users by name without exposing raw rows.

         Authoring permissions (separate concern):
         - A binding in alice/main is callable by anyone
           in the same image (read access implicit).
         - Only alice can edit bindings in alice/* branches.
         - main is owned by admin; merges require admin
           approval (default).

         This model is the Unix model, by choice. It maps
         cleanly to "SSH-first, multi-user OS" framing. If
         we wanted a Lisp-machine feel (shared trusted
         environment), we'd pick differently, but
         SSH-first implies Unix-style multi-user.

         SOCIAL LAYER (chat + plan) uses SHARED data_rows
         (existing storage rule) with handler-enforced
         filters by (whoami). That is intentional: messages
         and plans are multi-user objects, not personal-branch
         pins. Branch gardens stay code/UI; social state is
         rows. See multi-user.chat and multi-user.user-plan.
   |))
   )
  )
  (subsection chat
   (doc "Multi-user chat: site lobby + DMs so humans on the box can talk")
   (status planned)
   (implements libs/chat/lib.pp image/lib/http.ml)
   (verify
    (build "dune build image")
    (smoke "alice and bob post to /chat lobby; each sees both messages")
    (smoke "alice DM to bob visible to both; not to carol")
    (smoke "unauth /chat redirects login; public face does not leak messages")
    (smoke "(chat/say \"hi\") and (chat/inbox) work via /api/eval"))
   (details
    (doc (|
         ==========================================================
         0. WHY
         ==========================================================

         Multi-user is not only isolation — people on the same
         habitat need a way to talk. Chat is a first-class
         product surface (like notes/todo), not an external app.
         Agents authenticated as users may post too (same
         identity rules).

         ==========================================================
         1. SHAPE (v1)
         ==========================================================

         Two conversation kinds:

           A. LOBBY (site-wide room)
              room_id = "lobby" (singleton)
              All authenticated users can read/write.
              Default /chat UI.

           B. DM (direct message thread)
              room_id = canonical pair key:
                dm:alice:bob  where alice < bob lexicographically
              Participants = the two usernames only.
              List/create from /chat/dm or (chat/dm "bob").

         Out of scope v1:
           - Arbitrary named rooms / channels (phase 2)
           - WebSockets / SSE push (htmx poll is enough)
           - File attachments, reactions, threads, edit history
           - End-to-end encryption
           - Cross-deployment federation

         ==========================================================
         2. DATA (shared data_rows)
         ==========================================================

         Package libs/chat seeds:

           (deftype 'chat-room
             'kind          ; "lobby" | "dm"
             'room_id       ; "lobby" | "dm:a:b"
             'title         ; display
             'members       ; JSON array of usernames; lobby = []
             'created_by
             'created_at)   ; ISO string from Lisp or server

           (deftype 'chat-message
             'room_id
             'from          ; username (whoami at post)
             'body          ; plain text (escape on render)
             'created_at)

         data_rows are SHARED across branches (storage rule).
         Access control is in chat/* handlers:
           - post: from MUST equal (whoami); reject spoof
           - list lobby: any authenticated user
           - list dm: whoami is member
           - create dm room: both users must exist

         Retention v1: keep all messages (personal OS scale).
         Optional later: max list limit + archive.

         Seed: ensure lobby room row exists on load-library
         (idempotent create-if-missing).

         ==========================================================
         3. LISP API (libs/chat)
         ==========================================================

           (chat/say body)              ; post to lobby as whoami
           (chat/say-in room_id body)   ; post to room if allowed
           (chat/lobby [limit])         ; recent lobby messages
           (chat/history room_id [limit])
           (chat/dm peer)               ; ensure dm room; return room_id
           (chat/dms)                   ; rooms where whoami is member
           (chat/users)                 ; list usernames (for DM picker)
                                        ; needs thin OCaml prim
                                        ; (list-usernames) for any authed

         New OCaml prim if missing (image):
           (list-usernames) → VList of VStr
             authenticated only; usernames are public identifiers
             on the box (no email/admin leak).

         ==========================================================
         4. HTTP / HTMX UI
         ==========================================================

         Routes (private by default — auth required):

           GET  /chat              lobby page
           GET  /chat/partial      message list fragment (poll)
           POST /chat/say          body=text → append + refresh
           GET  /chat/dm           list DM threads
           GET  /chat/dm/:peer     DM thread with peer
           POST /chat/dm/:peer     send DM

         Polling: hx-get /chat/partial hx-trigger every 3s-5s.
         No WebSocket in v1.

         Chrome: ui/nav link Chat when package loaded.
         Render: escape body text; show from + time + body.
         Empty state: Say hi to the box.

         ==========================================================
         5. SECURITY / POLICY
         ==========================================================

         - No anonymous chat (kernel private routes)
         - Body max length (e.g. 4k chars) enforced in chat/say
         - Rate: soft — eval budget enough for v1
         - XSS: escape user body on render; never raw untrusted HTML
         - Spoof: ignore client-supplied from; always whoami

         ==========================================================
         6. PHASES
         ==========================================================

         Phase 1: deftype + lobby say/history + /chat UI poll
         Phase 2: DMs + list-usernames + /chat/dm
         Phase 3: nav seed, optional presence / unread

         Success: two browser sessions (alice, bob) talk in
         lobby without leaving the habitat; agent as alice can
         (chat/say "deployed") visible to bob.
    |))
   )
  )
  (subsection user-plan
   (doc "Unix-style .plan: short per-user status of what they intend to work on")
   (status planned)
   (implements libs/plan/lib.pp)
   (verify
    (build "dune build image")
    (smoke "(plan \"macros + chat\") sets alice plan; (plan-of \"alice\") returns it")
    (smoke "GET /u/alice shows plan text; bob can read alice plan")
    (smoke "only owner can set own plan; bob cannot set alice")
    (smoke "empty plan shows friendly placeholder"))
   (details
    (doc (|
         ==========================================================
         0. WHY (finger / .plan)
         ==========================================================

         Classic Unix: each user has a ~/.plan file; finger(1)
         shows it. On a multi-user Lisp OS this is the lightest
         coordination tool — not a task tracker (todo exists),
         not chat, just what I'm working on / intend next.

         Complements chat: plan is sticky status; chat is
         conversation.

         ==========================================================
         1. DATA
         ==========================================================

         (deftype 'user-plan
           'username      ; owner; unique logical key
           'body          ; free text (plain)
           'updated_at)   ; ISO string

         One logical plan per username. Implementation:
           find existing row where username matches whoami;
           update body + updated_at, or create if missing.
         Shared data_rows; filter by username field.

         Optional later: project / until date — not v1.

         ==========================================================
         2. LISP API (libs/plan)
         ==========================================================

           (plan body)           ; set MY plan (whoami); return body
           (plan)                ; get MY plan body or empty string
           (plan-of user)        ; get that user's plan body or empty
           (plan-clear)          ; set my body to empty
           (plan-list)           ; list of (username . body) for
                                 ; users with non-empty plans

         Spoof: (plan) always writes username=(whoami).
         There is no (plan-as other body).

         ==========================================================
         3. HTTP UI
         ==========================================================

         Private (auth required) unless noted:

           GET  /plan              edit-or-view MY plan form
           POST /plan              save body
           GET  /plans             directory: who has a plan set
           GET  /u/:username       profile stub: username +
                                   plan body + updated_at +
                                   link to garden subdomain if
                                   auth.user-subdomains on
                                   (finger alice)

         Public option (phase 2, with route-visibility):
           public /u/:user so plans can be world-readable like
           classic finger. v1: authenticated users on the box only.

         Chrome: show MY plan one-liner under whoami in header
         or on home (ui/home snippet) — optional phase 2.
         Nav: Plan → /plan; People → /plans.

         ==========================================================
         4. UX COPY
         ==========================================================

         Placeholder when empty:
           No plan yet. What are you working on?

         Form label:
           Your plan (like a Unix .plan file)

         Help text:
           Short note other users on this box can read.
           Examples: shipping macros, out until Thursday.

         ==========================================================
         5. PHASES
         ==========================================================

         Phase 1: deftype + plan/plan-of/plan-list + /plan + /u/:user
         Phase 2: /plans directory + home/chrome snippet
         Phase 3: optional public /u/:user; subdomain profile page

         Success: alice sets plan macros + multi-user chat;
         bob opens /u/alice and knows what she's doing; chat
         stays for the actual conversation.
    |))
   )
  )
 )
)
